  - hotspotId: sarang
    partName: Sarang
    localPosition: {x: 0.2800001, y: 0.25, z: -0.22882679}
    visibleFrom: 0
    title:
      id: Sarang Gundukan
      en: Mound nest
    regionalTerm: 
    material:
      id: Gundukan tanah & serasah buatan burung gosong kaki-jingga (Megapodius reinwardt);
        pada model dipotong dan diperkecil
      en: A soil-and-litter mound built by the orange-footed scrubfowl (Megapodius
        reinwardt); cut away and reduced on the model
    philosophy:
      id: '[Draf] Diisi kurator setelah spesimen asli ditetapkan.'
      en: '[Draft] To be written by the curator once the real specimen is chosen.'
    craft:
      id: Komodo betina meletakkan telurnya di lubang tanah, cekungan di lereng bukit,
        atau gundukan sarang burung gosong kaki-jingga yang telah ditinggalkan. Di
        Pulau Komodo, sekitar 62% sarang komodo adalah gundukan sarang burung gosong.
        Pada model, muka depan gundukan dipotong agar telur di dalamnya terlihat.
      en: Female Komodo dragons lay their eggs in holes in the ground, hollows in
        hillsides, or abandoned mounds built by the orange-footed scrubfowl. On Komodo
        Island about 62% of dragon nests are scrubfowl mounds. On the model the front
        of the mound is cut away so the eggs inside can be seen.
    history:
      id: '[Draf] Diisi kurator setelah spesimen asli ditetapkan.'
      en: '[Draft] To be written by the curator once the real specimen is chosen.'
    narrationID: {fileID: 0}
    narrationEN: {fileID: 0}
    pronunciation: {fileID: 0}
    transcript:
      id: Komodo betina meletakkan telurnya di lubang tanah, cekungan di lereng bukit,
        atau gundukan sarang burung gosong kaki-jingga yang telah ditinggalkan. Di
        Pulau Komodo, sekitar 62% sarang komodo adalah gundukan sarang burung gosong.
        Pada model, muka depan gundukan dipotong agar telur di dalamnya terlihat.
      en: Female Komodo dragons lay their eggs in holes in the ground, hollows in
        hillsides, or abandoned mounds built by the orange-footed scrubfowl. On Komodo
        Island about 62% of dragon nests are scrubfowl mounds. On the model the front
        of the mound is cut away so the eggs inside can be seen.
